cent research on bat vision. For instance, studies show that Egyptian fruit bats use vision to fly towards landmarks, and even some echolocating bats can see ultraviolet light. The consensus among zoologists today is crystal clear: No bat is naturally blind, and most have useful eyesight. So you didn’t mishear or misremember—it’s a case of a myth being powerful enough to sneak even into some educational programming. You’re right to question it, and now you know the more nuanced truth.
