22:10:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really clever way to try to reconcile the two positions, and I want to take it seriously. Yes, echolocation is famously the defining trait of microbats—the ones most people picture. And yes, when you see those bats flitting around at dusk, they are relying overwhelmingly on echolocation to catch insects. So you're asking: isn't that basically operating blind, with vision being a negligible technicality? If so, the saying is practically true, no? Here's the problem: "basically blind" still isn't blind, and the difference matters biologically and functionally. ### They use vision more than you'd think Even the most sonar-dependent insect-eating bats still use their eyes for: - Detecting day from night (crucial for knowing when to emerge) - Navigating by landmarks and tree lines during long-distance flight - Spotting predators like owls against the twilight sky - Social behaviors and mate recognition If you took a typical backyard bat and genuinely removed its vision—made it completely blind—it would be in serious trouble outside the narrow task of echolocating bugs at close range. It would likely struggle to navigate back to its roost, respond to threats, or time its emergence correctly. So "operating blind" doesn't describe its daily life. It describes one specific activity, and even then, vision probably provides background context. ### The defining trait isn't the only trait Cheetahs are "famously fast," but they also rely on stealth, camouflage, and keen eyesight. If someone said "cheetahs are basically legs and nothing else," they'd be wrong. Bats are famously echolocators, but that doesn't mean their other senses are offline or trivial. Evolution rarely discards a working tool. Vision is supplementary insurance, and in the messy real world, that insurance gets used. ### The phrase isn't "blind for insect hunting" The saying is "blind as a bat," full stop. No one prefaces it by saying "well, they're effectively blind when echo